[{:db/id 17592186362393 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "Interpret the positive quantity calculated below as the magnitude of the rate of change in the direction of fastest decrease.\n\nAt a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSigned-rate note: $\\nabla f(2,-1)=(1,1)$ and the unit direction of fastest decrease is $\\mathbf u=(-1,-1)/\\sqrt2$. Therefore $D_{\\mathbf u}f(2,-1)=\\nabla f(2,-1)\\cdot\\mathbf u=-\\sqrt2$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt2$, which is the positive quantity requested in the clarified study question." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
